SECTION 9  HYPOTHESIS (final wording; enrollment-vs-completion caveat included)
====================================================================================================
HYPOTHESIS - Black vs non-Black gap in BA-type attainment across SES levels
(nls_raw.csv = NLSY79 extract, 12,686 respondents, 61 columns)

MEASUREMENT LIMIT STATED UP FRONT. The file contains no degree/attainment variable whatsoever - no
"Highest degree received", no "Ba completion, 1992/1994/1996", and no ready-made SES score or poverty
ratio (verified by scanning column names AND all cell values; the 61 columns match the datasheet
exactly). BA DEGREE COMPLETION IS NOT MEASURABLE IN THIS FILE. All numbers below describe college
PARTICIPATION/ENROLLMENT, proxied as: respondent ever recorded in residence type 3 "DORM, FRATERNITY,
SORORITY" in any wave 1981-1996, or already >=16 grades completed in 1979. No claim about receiving a
degree is made.

CORE HYPOTHESIS. The Black/non-Black difference is not a fixed "advantage" or "deficit" but an SES-
conditional one, and the advantage exists only at the bottom of the socioeconomic distribution: Black
respondents from the lowest origin-SES families participate in college at rates statistically
indistinguishable from (and unconditionally even slightly above) non-Black non-Hispanic respondents
(4.0% vs 3.4%, +0.6pp, CI -0.9..+2.2), and once sex and ASVAB achievement are held constant that bottom
cell becomes a significant Black ADVANTAGE of +3.5pp (CI +1.8..+5.2, p=0.0001). That advantage shrinks
to zero at the middle SES level and reverses into a widening deficit at high SES (high tertile: 11.4% vs
19.3%, -7.9pp, CI -11.6..-3.0; adjusted net effect -6.4pp, p=0.006). Formally the gap steepens by
-7.6pp per +1 SD of the origin-SES index (LPM HC1 CI -9.0..-6.1) and by OR 0.46 per SD on the relative
scale (0.36-0.58, p=7e-11). Substantively: family socioeconomic status converts into college
participation far less efficiently for Black students than for non-Black non-Hispanic students, so any
Black advantage is confined to low-SES families and is extinguished, then reversed, as SES rises.

PRIMARY EVIDENCE (analytic n=10,671; BLACK 1,623 vs NON-BLACK NON-HISPANIC 6,770; SES tertiles built
only from family-of-origin variables that pre-date the outcome - "Occupation of adult male in household
at age 14" collapsed white/blue collar at code<500, mother's and father's "Highest grade completed",
and "Total net family income, previous calendar year, 1979" per capita (log), equal-weight z-mean;
comparison group fixed a priori; Hispanic shown separately):
