Black x SES-level interaction: logit joint Wald chi2=8.21, df=2, p=0.0165; per-term p=0.029 (mid) and
0.008 (high). Absolute-scale slope of the gap: -7.55pp per +1SD SES (CI -9.00..-6.10). Relative-scale
slope: OR 0.459 per +1SD (0.363-0.580, p=7e-11). Adjusted for sex and ASVAB word knowledge, net Black
effect = +3.46pp at low SES (p=0.0001), -0.18pp at mid (p=0.89), -6.36pp at high (p=0.006); adjusted
trend slope -7.78pp per SD (p=3e-22).
SCALE NOTE declared in advance: the ABSOLUTE gap widens monotonically (+0.6 -> -3.0 -> -7.9pp) while the
RELATIVE gap widens between the low and middle levels and then flattens (RR 1.19 -> 0.59 -> 0.59). Both
scales agree in sign of the trend; the two statements are reported side by side, neither is chosen post
hoc. Hispanic respondents behave differently again (higher participation than the non-Black
non-Hispanic comparison at mid/high SES), which is why they were not pooled into the comparison group.

ROBUSTNESS (pattern reproduced in every specification): occupation white/blue cut at 400 / 600 instead
of 500 (high-SES gap -9.0 / -8.3pp); quartiles instead of tertiles (+0.8, -2.6, -1.7, -10.4pp); SES
index without occupation at all (+0.7, -3.0, -8.1pp); attrition-robust index keeping >=2 of 4 components
(Black retention 94.8% instead of 81.1%; +0.2, -2.2, -7.4pp); complete-case sample (low -0.5, mid -1.4,
high -8.9pp); pure dorm outcome without the >=16-grade top-up (37 added cases in total; high -8.1pp);
comparison = all non-Black (-0.4, -4.5, -8.2pp); age strata addressing right-censoring (age 14-16 in
1979, whose college entry falls inside the dorm-rich 1983-85 waves: +0.8, -1.3, -17.4pp; age 18-22,
window closed by 1986: -1.2, -0.2, -5.0pp; age 20-22: -2.2, +2.2, -4.2pp); and the strictly time-aligned
risk set restricted to HS-completed-by-1979 (-1.5, +2.0, -2.0pp; only 24 Black events, trend slope
-2.6pp per SD, p=0.02 - direction preserved, magnitude not estimable).

PRECISION BUDGET / WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. Low tertile: post-hoc power 0.13 for the observed +0.6pp and an
80%-power MDE of ~2.1pp, so the unadjusted "no gap at low SES" means "not distinguishable from zero",
not demonstrated parity (the +3.5pp adjusted advantage is a separate, powered statement). High tertile:
237 Black respondents with 27 events, MDE 7.5pp, so the level-specific estimate is indicative; the
pooled SES x race trend is the well-powered result. Direction, not point magnitude, is the claim. Black
cells are small because Black respondents are concentrated in low SES (61% of Black analytic sample in
the bottom tertile vs 22% of the comparison group) and because all Black respondents come from
oversample strata with no sampling weights in the file - nothing here is nationally representative.

CAVEATS. (1) The outcome is college ENROLLMENT measured through dormitory/fraternity residence, not BA
completion; students who attended college from the parental home are missed, so both levels and the gap
are proxy-dependent and nothing can be said about degree receipt or timing. (2) Origin-SES tertiles
cannot separate race from unmeasured parental wealth, neighbourhood or discrimination channels; the
analysis documents the SES-conditional gap, it does not identify its cause. (3) Respondent's own "Family
net wealth" 1985/1990/1996 and "Total net family income" 1985/1989 are far lower for Black respondents
(median wealth ratio 0.46 / 0.37 / 0.23, Mann-Whitney p<1e-10) but they are post-outcome adult
resources; they are reported as context and excluded from the SES index. Forcing them in (over-
adjustment) makes the low-SES cell a significant -1.9pp deficit and barely changes the high-SES gap
(-7.7pp), i.e. it conditions on a downstream consequence of the pathway under study. (4) Missingness is
race-graded (parental occupation missing 34.3% Black vs 22.4% comparison; logistic OR for Black
=1.46, 1.28-1.67, p=2e-8; father's grades 18.2% vs 8.1%), so complete-case is a sensitivity only and the
index-based "mean of available components" is the primary treatment of missingness. (5) Residence
coverage in the key 1981-86 window is near-universal (97.9%; Black 97.6%), so the proxy is not
differentially missing by race in the enrollment window, but its dorm-only definition remains a
substantive approximation.
